Build dependencies
==================
Translation catalogs are external runtime files. Restore NotQuiteRSS_*.ts into
resources/external/translations/ before running qmake to build them with the matching Qt lrelease tool.
Precompiled .qm files can also be installed or added by users without rebuilding.
See resources/external/translations/README.md for discovery paths, optional metadata and installation.

  C++17-capable GCC or Clang and make (Windows recipe: MSYS2 UCRT64 GCC)
  SQLite 3.36.0 or newer with the memdb VFS, including development headers and sqlite3.pc
  libxml2 development headers and libxml-2.0.pc
  pkg-config (pkgconf is supported)
  Qt 5.15.x (default) or Qt 6.2+ development packages and matching qmake
  Qt modules: Core, Gui, Widgets, PrintSupport, Network, Sql, Xml, Svg
  Qt's QSQLITE plugin (used for initialization and VACUUM)
  Qt 6 additionally requires Core5Compat for the existing QTextCodec decoders

Selecting Qt 5 or Qt 6
=====================
The application defaults to Qt 5. Invoke qmake from the matching Qt installation:

  qmake-qt5 ../app.pro CONFIG+=release CONFIG-=debug_and_release
  qmake6 ../app.pro USE_QT=6 CONFIG+=release CONFIG-=debug_and_release

USE_QT accepts only 5 or 6; a mismatch with qmake's Qt is an error.
It cannot switch the libraries used by an already selected qmake executable.
The platform recipes below retain Qt 5 defaults. Only Windows CI selects Qt 6.

For Qt 6 on MSYS2, substitute qt6-base, qt6-svg, qt6-tools and
qt6-imageformats for the qt5 packages below, and add
mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-qt6-5compat. Then use the Qt 6 application command above.
Deploy with windeployqt6; the Qt 5 --no-angle option is not applicable.

On Linux, install the equivalent Qt 6 development packages, including
Core5Compat. On Homebrew, select the Qt 6 formula's bin directory instead of
qt@5 and pass USE_QT=6.
Executable names vary by distribution; an absolute path to qmake is also valid.

Both builds read/write UTF-8 INI values so non-ASCII settings survive switching
between them. Existing Qt 5-generated INI files remain readable. To return to
an older, unpatched Qt 5 build after running Qt 6, use a settings backup: older
builds without an explicit UTF-8 INI codec may misread non-ASCII values.

Notification audio
==================
The application uses system audio players (paplay, afplay, aplay) for WAV files.
A working audio service/device is required; codec plugins are not needed.
Supported players are detected automatically: paplay (PulseAudio/PipeWire) on
Linux, afplay on macOS, aplay (ALSA) as fallback on Linux. When no player is
available, playback failures are logged silently.

Article age retention
=====================
With shutdown cleanup and its maximum-age option enabled, ordinary RSS/Atom
updates ignore entries older than the publication-age cutoff. The first
successful fetch of a new feed is exempt; emptying an existing feed is not.
On shutdown, expired dated articles are physically removed, including old
duplicate records. Existing unread/starred/labeled protections still apply
to articles that have not already been cleaned up.

Count-based cleanup keeps duplicate records until they expire by age. Entries
without a usable publisher date retain receipt-age cleanup and duplicate
records. Legacy synthetic receipt timestamps are treated conservatively as
undated. The manual cleanup wizard retains its independent receipt-age rules.
Changing the age window later cannot restore already removed articles, but a
subsequent feed update can import entries allowed by the new window.

Article sharing data
====================
The installed application reads social-networks/configuration.ini and icons
from the same social-networks directory under its resource root. On Linux with
PREFIX=/usr this is /usr/share/NotQuiteRSS/social-networks/. The shared qmake
resource stage installs both the configuration and all service icons.

Only when the installed INI file does not exist, the application reads
~/.config/NotQuiteRSS/social-networks/configuration.ini on Linux (the equivalent
social-networks subdirectory beside the application INI on other platforms). The
user definition is then used in full; installed and user definitions are never
merged. Startup diagnostics use the [article-sharing] prefix in debug.log when
file logging is enabled. Launch with --debug to also print diagnostics to stderr.

The INI [services] order value lists service section names. Each section accepts
name, icon, url, enabled and an optional action name used to retain shortcut and
toolbar compatibility. URL templates support the percent-encoded placeholders
{url} and {title}. Icons must be plain filenames within social-networks/.

SQLite and libxml2 are required external libraries on EVERY platform. qmake
uses pkg-config for include paths, library paths and transitive link flags.
Set PKG_CONFIG_PATH to the directories containing sqlite3.pc and libxml-2.0.pc
for a non-default installation. On Windows use native paths with forward
slashes (C:/...) and the native UCRT64 pkg-config, not /usr/bin/pkg-config.
Do not mix architectures, MSVC/MinGW, or MSYS2 environments in one build.

SQLitex in 3rdparty/sqlitex remains the application's custom Qt SQL driver.
It and the direct SQLite backup calls link to the same external SQLite engine.
Do not substitute Qt's QSQLITE plugin for SQLitex. Use a normal thread-safe
SQLite build with UTF-16, declared column types and the backup API enabled.
The old SQLITE_OMIT_* defines applied only to the deleted engine sources;
application compiler flags cannot configure a prebuilt SQLite library.

Linux (Fedora 44)
================
  sudo dnf install python3 gcc-c++ make pkgconf-pkg-config qt5-qtbase-devel \
    qt5-qtsvg-devel qt5-qttools-devel \
    sqlite-devel libxml2-devel qt5-qtimageformats
  mkdir _build
  cd _build
  qmake-qt5 ../app.pro CONFIG+=release CONFIG-=debug_and_release PREFIX=/usr
  make -j2
  sudo make install

Other Linux distributions need the equivalent development packages and Qt 5
toolchain. qmake may be named qmake or qmake-qt5. For Clang on Linux,
install clang and lld, then pass -spec linux-clang and QMAKE_LFLAGS+=-fuse-ld=lld
to qmake so compiler-specific LTO flags match the compiler and linker.

Windows (MSYS2 UCRT64, x86-64)
=============================
Install MSYS2 from https://www.msys2.org/ and fully update it with pacman -Syu
(follow its restart instructions). Use the UCRT64 shell for ALL commands below.
The recipe uses package-managed Qt, SQLite, libxml2 and their runtime libraries;
it does not use the Qt online installer's separate MinGW or an MSVC toolchain.

  pacman -S --needed git \
    mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-gcc mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-make \
    mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-python mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-pkgconf \
    mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-qt5-base mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-qt5-svg mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-qt5-tools \
    mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-qt5-imageformats \
    mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-sqlite3 mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-libxml2 \
    mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-openssl

From the application source directory:
  export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$(cygpath -m /ucrt64/lib/pkgconfig)"
  mkdir _build
  cd _build
  qmake-qt5 ../app.pro CONFIG+=release CONFIG-=debug_and_release
  mingw32-make -j2
  mingw32-make install
  ./release/target/notquiterss.exe

The install target copies the shared external resource stage into resources/
beside the executable, including styles, sounds, translations and sharing data. The UCRT64 shell supplies the dependency DLL paths for local
testing. MSVC is not covered by this recipe or CI: it needs compatible external
libraries; do not link the UCRT64 C++ archive into an MSVC application.

macOS (Homebrew, native architecture)
====================================
Install Xcode Command Line Tools and Homebrew (https://brew.sh/) first.
  brew install qt sqlite libxml2 pkgconf python

From the application source directory:
  export PATH="$(brew --prefix qt)/bin:$PATH"
  export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$(brew --prefix sqlite)/lib/pkgconfig:$(brew --prefix libxml2)/lib/pkgconfig"
  mkdir _build
  cd _build
  qmake ../app.pro USE_QT=6 CONFIG+=release CONFIG-=debug_and_release
  make -j2
  open release/target/NotQuiteRSS.app

This recipe uses Homebrew Qt 6.
The explicit pkg-config paths select
Homebrew SQLite/libxml2 rather than SDK libraries. Use one native Homebrew
architecture consistently; this recipe does not produce a universal binary.

macOS CI package
================
The macos job builds a native Apple Silicon (arm64) Qt 6 application on macos-15,
deploys Qt/plugins and linked dylibs with macdeployqt, applies an ad-hoc code
signature, and uploads a compressed DMG as a GitHub Actions artifact (14 days).
Download it from the workflow run's Artifacts section and extract the DMG.
The package is not universal and does not support Intel Macs. Its macOS minimum
is constrained by the Homebrew libraries bundled by that run; older macOS
versions are not validated by CI.

No signing secrets are required. These beta packages are not Developer ID signed
or notarized, so Gatekeeper may require approval through Privacy & Security.
The job checks for required Cocoa/SQLite plugins, remaining absolute non-system
library dependencies, signature validity and DMG integrity. It does not launch
the application or publish a GitHub Release. Test a downloaded package on a Mac
without Homebrew/Qt, including HTTPS feeds, images, sound and database access.

Configuration and deployment notes
==================================
If qmake reports a missing dependency, verify in the SAME build environment:
  qmake -query QT_VERSION
  pkg-config --modversion sqlite3 libxml-2.0
  pkg-config --cflags --libs sqlite3 libxml-2.0
  pkg-config --atleast-version=3.36.0 sqlite3
No application configuration/build step downloads dependencies.

These recipes and CI compile for local use; they do not create self-contained
release packages. For redistribution, use the matching windeployqt/macdeployqt
on release/target/notquiterss.exe or release/target/NotQuiteRSS.app, then inspect and
deploy non-Qt dependencies as well (SQLite, libxml2 and their dependencies,
OpenSSL/TLS backend, compiler runtime where needed). Deployment tools alone
must not be assumed to collect every non-Qt library. macOS bundles need their
non-system dylib paths fixed and signing performed after all files are copied.

Keep the QSQLITE plugin, Qt platform plugin and image-format
plugins, including WebP. Linux packages should depend on the selected Qt's
image-format package (qt5-qtimageformats for Qt 5 on Fedora), or its distribution
equivalent at runtime. Without WebP support the reader does
not advertise WebP but cannot display images available only in that format.
Provide the matching TLS runtime and a usable certificate trust store; verify
HTTPS feeds and article images on the target machine. Do not disable certificate
verification to work around deployment mistakes.

After a build, check database creation/reopening, in-memory database save/restore,
Unicode sorting and REGEXP, a second-instance launch, HTTPS feeds and images,
notifications and printing/export. Windows/macOS builds still
need these runtime checks; compilation alone cannot establish them.

Application styles
==================
The normal install/bundle targets deploy the style directory on every platform.
